<section class="sec"><div class="wrap"><div class="prose"><p>A television programme is a chain of a few hundred decisions, and most of them are made before the first camera is switched on. <strong>I'm Sandra Pudja, and I produce TV formats end to end</strong>: format development and pitch documents, scripting and rundowns, guest booking and talent handling, studio and set logistics, floor and gallery direction on the day, and the handover into post-production.</p><p>I work with broadcasters, production companies and brands in Berlin and across Europe, in English, German and Croatian. Typical formats: weekly studio magazines, entertainment specials, award shows, talk formats and multi-camera branded shows for streaming.</p><p>What clients notice first is that nothing on my productions is left to luck. <strong>The call sheet goes out the evening before. Every item in the rundown has an owner and a time.</strong> And when something does break at 9:14, the show still starts at 9:15, because plan B had a plan C.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="sec"><div class="wrap"><h2>What you get</h2><div class="grid3"><div class="card"><span class="mono">01</span><h3>Format and concept</h3><p>A format bible, pitch deck and a realistic budget before anyone books a studio.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="mono">02</span><h3>Pre-production</h3><p>Script, rundown, guest booking, set and graphics brief, crew and studio booking.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="mono">03</span><h3>Production day</h3><p>Floor and gallery coordination, timing, talent handling, live problem solving.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="mono">04</span><h3>Post handover</h3><p>Edit brief, assets, logs and a wrap report so the editor starts on Monday, not Wednesday.</p></div></div></div></section>
<section class="sec"><div class="wrap"><h2>How it works</h2><div class="grid3"><div class="card"><h3>1. Brief</h3><p>A 30-minute call: what, for whom, when, and what budget actually exists.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>2. Plan</h3><p>Within a week you get a rundown, a schedule and a cost estimate you can sign off.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>3. Produce</h3><p>I run the floor. You watch the monitor.</p></div></div></div></section>
<section class="sec"><div class="wrap"><h2>Questions people ask</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Do you produce in German?</summary><p>Yes. I work in English, German and Croatian and run German-language studio productions in Berlin.</p></details><details><summary>Do you work with in-house teams?</summary><p>Mostly. I slot into existing broadcaster or agency teams as producer, line producer or production manager.</p></details><details><summary>Which formats are typical?</summary><p>Weekly studio magazines, entertainment and award specials, talk formats, multi-camera branded content for streaming.</p></details><details><summary>How early should we book?</summary><p>For a studio show, six to eight weeks before the first recording day. For a one-off special, four weeks is workable.</p></details></div></div></section>
